L17@P0: Abstract
L18@P0: We propose risk-sensitive reinforcement learning algorithms catering to three families of risk
L19@P0: measures, namely expectiles, utility-based shortfall risk and optimized certainty equivalent risk.
L20@P0: For each risk measure, in the context of a finite horizon Markov decision process, we first derive
L21@P0: a policy gradient theorem. Second, we propose estimators of the risk-sensitive policy gradient for
L22@P0: each of the aforementioned risk measures, and establish O (1/m) mean-squared error bounds for our
L23@P0: estimators, where m is the number of trajectories. Further, under standard assumptions for policy
L24@P0: gradient-type algorithms, we establish smoothness of the risk-sensitive objective, in turn leading to
L25@P0: stationary convergence rate bounds for the overall risk-sensitive policy gradient algorithm that we
L26@P0: propose. Finally, we conduct numerical experiments to validate the theoretical findings on popular
L27@P0: RL benchmarks.
L28@P0: 1 Introduction
L29@P0: In recent years, risk-sensitive reinforcement learning (RL) has received increased research attention, cf.
L30@P0: [5, 6, 21, 22, 23, 26, 28, 33] for some representative works. The shortcomings of expected value as an
L31@P0: objective for decision making and the need for incorporating risk is well-known, especially in domains such
L32@P0: as finance, robotics, and transportation. Several risk measures have been studied in an RL framework,
L33@P0: e.g., variance, quantiles, Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR), and cumulative prospect theory.
L34@P0: In this paper, we consider three risk measures, namely expectiles [3, 24], utility-based shortfall risk
L35@P0: (UBSR) [12] and optimized certainty equivalent (OCE) [4] risk. Each of these risk measures comes under
L36@P0: the realm of convex risk measures, which in financial parlance ensures that diversification does not increase
L37@P0: risk. UBSR and OCE constitute a rich class of risk measures with several interesting special cases, namely
L38@P0: entropic risk, quadratic, CVaR, optimal net-present value (ONPV) [25], mean-variance and quartic risks
L39@P0: [17], see Table 2 in Appendix A. For a textbook treatment of risk measures and their properties that
L40@P0: capture the attitude towards risk, the reader is referred to [13].
L41@P0: A prominent solution approach for optimizing risk measures in an RL context involves the use of
L42@P0: policy gradient algorithms, see [29]. We now summarize our contributions for each class of risk measures
L43@P0: that we study.
