L46@P0-1: arXiv:2602.09300v1 [cs.LG] 10 Feb 2026Expectiles. First, we derive mean-squared error and high probability bounds for the classic estimator
L47@P1: of expectiles from independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) samples. Second, we derive both lower
L48@P1: and upper bounds for estimating an expectile of the cumulative discounted cost in a Markov cost process.
L49@P1: Third, we derive an analogue of the policy gradient theorem albeit with an expectile objective for a
L50@P1: finite-horizon MDP. Using this theorem, we form an estimator of the expectile gradient using sample
L51@P1: trajectories of the underlying MDP and establish mean-squared error bounds for our proposed estimator.
L52@P1: UBSR and OCE. Unlike expectiles, estimation of UBSR and OCE from i.i.d. samples has been
L53@P1: studied in the literature, see [17, 18]. In this work, we focus on the policy gradient approach for UBSR and
L54@P1: OCE. Towards this goal, we first derive policy gradient theorem analogues for UBSR and OCE objectives
L55@P1: in a finite horizon MDP. This result is then employed to arrive at UBSR and OCE gradient estimators
L56@P1: using trajectories simulated from the underlying MDP.
L57@P1: Risk-aware policy gradient. We analyze a general policy gradient algorithm for optimizing a
L58@P1: risk measure under the following assumptions: (i) the objective is smooth; and (ii) the MSE of gradient
L59@P1: estimators is bounded above by O(1/m), where m is the number of trajectories. We establish convergence
L60@P1: of this template algorithm to an approximate first-order stationary point in the non-asymptotic regime.
L61@P1: Next, we show that expectiles, UBSR and OCE satisfy the aforementioned assumptions, and hence, the
L62@P1: bounds for the template algorithm can be specialized to these risk measures.
L63@P1: Experiments. We implemented REINFORCE [37] and our risk-aware policy gradient algorithms
L64@P1: on Reacher MuJoCo environment. From our experiments, we observe that expectile and UBSR with a
L65@P1: quadratic loss function outperform REINFORCE by exhibiting better average rewards and lower variance.
L66@P1: Related work. Expectiles have been studied for several decades, see [1, 2, 3, 8, 24, 34, 39]. However,
L67@P1: to the best of our knowledge, bounds for expectile estimation in the i.i.d. and Markovian cases have not
L68@P1: been derived in the literature and our work fills this research gap. Moreover, in the RL framework, there
L69@P1: is limited exploration of expectiles barring a few exceptions, see [7, 20, 30]. In [7], the authors formulate
L70@P1: a expectile variant of the Bellman equation and derive algorithms for finding a policy that solves this
L71@P1: equation. In [20, 30], distributional RL is studied with expectiles. The aforementioned references do not
L72@P1: consider a policy gradient approach for optimizing expectiles and the policy obtained by their algorithms
L73@P1: do not necessarily optimize the expectile of the cumulative cost random variable.
L74@P1: While UBSR and OCE estimation has been explored well in the literature, see [10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 27],
L75@P1: there is limited research that integrates UBSR and OCE within a RL framework with the exception of
L76@P1: [32, 38]. In [32], the authors study a nested variant of UBSR in a discounted MDP. As in the case of
L77@P1: expectiles, the policy that optimizes nested UBSR is not necessarily optimal for UBSR applied directly
L78@P1: to the cumulative cost random variable. We optimize the latter objective using policy gradients. In
L79@P1: [38], the authors consider iterative algorithms for the episodic risk-sensitive RL problems using the OCE
L80@P1: risk measure, and obtain regret bounds in a model-based setting. In contrast, we consider the model
L81@P1: free setting, and derive a policy gradient theorem for OCE, and obtain non-asymptotic bounds on the
L82@P1: convergence of the policy parameters.
